slides.body:
“Only show completed lessons” leaves questions unanswered: Does completion mean visited or explicitly marked? Are quizzes included? What about missing completion data?

An authored JavaScript example makes one contract explicit:

```js
function getCompletedLessons(lessons) {
  return lessons.filter(lesson => lesson.completed === true);
}
```

This treats only the boolean true as completed; it does not accept the string "true".

Jeffries describes a small abstraction around a recurring search. It can start with a simple implementation and later change the underlying collection without changing each caller. The boundary also keeps the API focused on the searches that are actually needed.

Authored example:

```js
function findLessonById(lessons, lessonId) {
  return lessons.find(lesson => lesson.id === lessonId);
}
```

Define what happens when no lesson matches before swapping the backing implementation.

Authored example, not a book listing. We search in array order, return the first matching object, return undefined if absent, and do not mutate the list. Inputs are an array of lesson records and an ID.

```js
function f(a, x) {
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i].id === x) return a[i];
  }
  return undefined;
}
```

The main problem is unclear intent. Do not silently add validation or a different not-found result while renaming it.

Authored refactoring:

```js
function findLessonById(lessons, lessonId) {
  for (let index = 0; index < lessons.length; index++) {
    if (lessons[index].id === lessonId) {
      return lessons[index];
    }
  }
  return undefined;
}
```

The function now tells a reader what the search means. The result and order are unchanged. Keeping the loop for this step makes the transformation easy to review.

Authored checks:

```js
const first = { id: "lesson-1", title: "Care" };
const laterDuplicate = { id: "lesson-1", title: "Other" };
const lessons = [first, laterDuplicate];
console.assert(findLessonById(lessons, "lesson-1") === first);
console.assert(findLessonById(lessons, "missing") === undefined);
console.assert(findLessonById([], "lesson-1") === undefined);
console.assert(lessons[0] === first && lessons.length === 2);
```

These cases cover first-match, missing, empty and unchanged input. They do not claim support for invalid inputs.

```js
function findLessonById(lessons, lessonId) {
  return lessons.find(lesson => lesson.id === lessonId);
}
```

find returns the first match, or undefined. A Map constructed with repeated keys normally keeps the last value, so it could return laterDuplicate. That would change the stated contract even though the implementation looked cleaner.
